\subsection{Representational Diversity Analysis}
\label{sec:cka_analysis}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig4_cka_heatmap.pdf}
    \caption{Layer-wise CKA similarity between dense and pruned representations for each method (28 layers, diagonal CKA). CKA-only produces the lowest dense similarity ($\mu{=}0.62$), confirming that it removes redundant structures and diverges most from the dense model. Standard ($\mu{=}0.90$), Random ($\mu{=}0.92$), and \rasp{} ($\mu{=}0.88$) all remain close to the dense representation.}
    \label{fig:cka_heatmap}
\end{figure}

To understand why CKA-only pruning enables recovery while other methods collapse, we measure CKA similarity between the dense and pruned model at each of the 28 transformer layers (Figure~\ref{fig:cka_heatmap}).
CKA-only produces the lowest mean diagonal CKA ($\mu{=}0.62$, range 0.38--0.99), while Standard ($\mu{=}0.90$), Random ($\mu{=}0.92$), and \rasp{} ($\mu{=}0.88$) all cluster near 1.0.
This inversion is the key insight: CKA-only removes structures with high pairwise overlap, which are by definition similar to retained structures.
The pruned model therefore \emph{diverges} most from the dense model in representational space, yet recovers best under RL.
Internal representational diversity and dense fidelity are not aligned: the method that preserves the most functionally unique structures is also the one that departs most from the original representation.
Standard and \rasp{} preserve dense fidelity by retaining important or composite-scored structures, but this fidelity comes at the cost of redundant representations that provide no exploration benefit during GRPO.


\subsection{Extended Training Cannot Rescue Collapse}
\label{sec:extended_training}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig5_extended_training.pdf}
    \caption{Standard pruning trained for 300 GRPO steps (3$\times$ the original budget). (a)~Reward plateaus at 0.054--0.058 with zero net gain beyond step 100, while CKA-only reaches 0.354 in 100 steps. (b)~Standard $f_0$ remains in the collapse zone ($>$0.5) for all 300 steps, confirming that additional training cannot rescue structurally damaged models.}
    \label{fig:extended_training}
\end{figure}

A natural question is whether the collapse observed in Standard pruning (\S\ref{sec:recovery_gradient}) can be rescued with additional training.
We extend Standard GRPO training from 100 to 300 steps under identical hyperparameters (Figure~\ref{fig:extended_training}).
Reward plateaus at 0.054--0.058 across steps 100--300, with the final step-300 reward (0.056) showing zero net improvement over step 100 (0.054).
The fraction of zero-variance batches ($f_0$) remains above 0.5 for all 300 steps, confirming persistent collapse: more than half of training batches produce no learning signal throughout the extended run.
By contrast, CKA-only achieves 0.354 peak reward in just 100 steps.
This result demonstrates that the recovery capacity gap is structural, not a matter of training budget.
Extended training cannot compensate for the loss of representationally unique structures that importance-based pruning removes.


\subsection{Cross-Scale Generalization}
\label{sec:cross_scale}

\input{tables/tab5_cross_scale}

To test whether the recovery capacity gradient generalizes beyond 7B parameters, we replicate the comparison on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B at 20\% structured sparsity with identical GRPO configuration (Table~\ref{tab:cross_scale}).
CKA-only achieves 0.238 peak reward on 1.5B, compared to 0.084 for Standard and 0.099 for \rasp{}.
The 2.4$\times$ CKA-only advantage over \rasp{} at 1.5B (vs 5.8$\times$ at 7B) suggests that the gradient persists across scales, though the absolute gap narrows at smaller model sizes where fewer redundant structures exist.
The ordering CKA-only $>$ \rasp{} $>$ Standard is preserved at 1.5B, matching the 7B pattern.


